Dude, for a while I was
doing the accounting.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:40.323
- [Gary] Not good.

00:09:40.323 --> 00:09:43.783
- I thought cash flow and
revenue were the same thing.

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:45.140
I be like guys we got to get these people

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:46.660
put that money in our
bank, that's not revenue.

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:47.870
I'm not paying your quota on that.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:49.063
- [Gary] I get it.

00:09:49.063 --> 00:09:50.563
- And you learn all that right stuff,

00:09:50.563 --> 00:09:53.010
the right way to do it,
that's what I'm referring to.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:53.890
- I totally understand.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.670
- You know how it is, like
not coming from this area

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.490
it's really uncomfortable at first,

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.707
but it's actually a
advantage in the end I think.

00:10:02.707 --> 00:10:04.053
- [Gary] 100,000%.

00:10:04.053 --> 00:10:06.730
- I used to have this book
club called the Anti MBA,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:08.420
because when I moved to San Francisco

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:09.930
I was so jealous of all
these Stanford people

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:10.960
so I would create this book club

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:11.970
and we'd meet once a week

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.020
and then we'd get experts to
come in and lead the discussion

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.989
and I was like look, we just
did the same thing as Stanford.

00:10:16.989 --> 00:10:18.033
- [Gary] Of course.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:20.479
- But yeah it's really
uncomfortable I think.

00:10:20.479 --> 00:10:21.530
You know how it is.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.280
- What are you thinking
about going forward?

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.539
Like where's your mindset

00:10:24.539 --> 00:10:29.240
on like the next half
decade, decade, next year,

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.189
like as an operator, as a
founder, as an entrepreneur

00:10:33.189 --> 00:10:35.169
what's exciting you right now?

00:10:35.169 --> 00:10:37.840
- Our business is doing well.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:40.240
I mean I don't necessarily
care about money

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.190
but I'm a runner, I ran in college

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:44.240
and so I love having goals.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.780
I want to hit 100 million
in revenue by 2025

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.160
and I'm really excited.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.900
I think there's a lot of
media brands out there

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:52.104
who operate horribly and
aren't doing the right things.

00:10:52.104 --> 00:10:54.930
I think we can build a massive
B2B publishing business.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.294
- Explain to me on that,

00:10:56.294 --> 00:10:58.089
like what are your hot takes on the--

00:10:58.089 --> 00:11:01.140
- I think the New York
media scene is horrible.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:01.990
- [Gary] Because?

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.990
- I think that they look
at Google Analytics more

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.060
than actually talk to
users and talk to customers

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.097
and figure out like what people want

00:11:07.097 --> 00:11:08.680
and they create shit they love.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.000
They don't treat them like customers,

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:11.010
it's just Google Analytics.

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.990
It's like oh, we just
change this headline to this

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.350
that's gonna do, it's like
what does that really mean

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:16.640
and what you're seeing now is

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:19.050
that's actually impacting
people in the long run.

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:20.610
This is why Bar Stool Sports sold for,

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.100
their stock just went up by 50%.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.640
Their sale price was effectively now

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.750
like 650 million dollars, right?

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:30.710
And Dave doesn't know shit I
bet at first about analytics.

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.080
- [Gary] No, he's an entrepreneur.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:33.740
- He just made stuff that people loved,

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:35.073
whereas a lot of the big companies,

00:11:35.073 --> 00:11:37.840
what are now big, they
don't do that as much

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.890
and I think that that's
a huge weakness for them

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:40.920
and will help us.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.633
- So non consumer centric, quant centric.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:45.860
- Yeah.
- Math.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:46.800
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.130
Which you can have a little bit of that.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:49.963
- [Gary] Agree.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.280
- Vice, what are they valued at?

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.661
Like two or three billion dollars.

00:11:55.661 --> 00:11:57.820
- Yeah I mean, I think
when you talk about Vice

00:11:57.820 --> 00:11:59.510
you have to talk about the narrative.

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:02.881
I mean one of the variables
in this whole game is,

00:12:02.881 --> 00:12:06.740
I don't know the story
so I can't speak to it,

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:08.951
but my perception from the outside is

00:12:08.951 --> 00:12:11.300
some of the founders and investors in Vice

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:13.450
did extremely well because--

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:15.320
- Yeah, I saw Shane
Smith sitting front row

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.730
at that Tyson Fury fight.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.660
- Yeah I mean, that's a very big part of

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:22.500
VC-backed businesses over
the last half decade,

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.960
that will be the legacy
of this 10-year window,

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.610
which is what was the
delta between building

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:28.963
long-term businesses,

00:12:29.911 --> 00:12:32.790
how much leverage did founders have

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.416
to be able to take money off the table

00:12:34.416 --> 00:12:36.886
at Series B and Series C levels,

00:12:36.886 --> 00:12:39.343
was that what they were
actually going for.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.170
Shane had an again, just
not knowing a lot about Vice

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:46.003
but one thing I definitely knew

00:12:46.003 --> 00:12:48.350
because I was in the
circles of medium brands,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:51.890
Shane had a remarkable ability to sell--

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:53.505
- So good.
- Big brands,

00:12:53.505 --> 00:12:57.970
big budgets, so you know
there's so many, I'm with you--

00:12:57.970 --> 00:12:59.295
- So here's the difference is,

00:12:59.295 --> 00:13:02.023
let's say Vice posts something
like on their social channel

00:13:02.023 --> 00:13:03.900
that says our top reporter is

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.210
gonna be here tomorrow, show up.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.280
Now let's say Gary posts
I'm gonna be here tomorrow,

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.863
show up, who's gonna have the most pull.

00:13:09.863 --> 00:13:10.760
- [Gary] Of course.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.300
- Right, yet they have a billion dollars

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.696
or 800 million dollars in
funding or something like that.

00:13:15.696 --> 00:13:16.529
- [Gary] Yep.

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:18.260
- That's to me is my point.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.770
- Are you self-funded for this business?

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:21.210
- We took a little bit of angel money

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:22.565
so I had self-funded it.

00:13:22.565 --> 00:13:25.900
The first couple conferences
I made 500 grand in profit

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:27.616
and I piled all that money into this,

00:13:27.616 --> 00:13:30.865
then we took small
checks from Tim Ferriss,

00:13:30.865 --> 00:13:32.510
the founders of Nerd Wallet,

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:34.023
the founders of Bleach Report.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:36.893
People like that.

00:13:36.893 --> 00:13:38.560
- People that you thought
could bring value.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.310
- Yeah because I was like
emailing these people

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:41.440
and I would ask them questions

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:42.273
and they're like can I invest?

00:13:42.273 --> 00:13:43.770
I'm like wait, you're gonna give me money

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.770
and I can just text you
questions whenever I want?

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:47.634
So we did a little bit of
that but no venture capital.

00:13:47.634 --> 00:13:51.273
- What's the best piece
of advice you've gotten

00:13:51.273 --> 00:13:53.900
from either smart money or outside people

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:56.100
or you consumed somewhere in the last,

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:57.370
let's call the last year?

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:58.690
- Just some personal advice,

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:00.540
Joe Speiser who started LittleThings.com,

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:02.710
which eventually went out,
but he's very successful.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.560
He was like I'm gonna write you this check

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.660
but just know I just bought
a big house in the Hamptons,

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.007
it's not gonna make you happy.

00:14:08.007 --> 00:14:11.760
And so like he gave me that
right when he gave us the money.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:13.360
What are some other good advice?

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:17.720
Tim Ferriss has been really helpful.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:21.330
I mean he's like anal
retentive about his brand

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:22.450
and about everything like that.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:23.430
- [Gary] He is, he's super thoughtful.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.760
- I think there's a
little middle ground there

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:29.740
but like just seeing like how
strict he is with his brand

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:31.410
and it totally works for him

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.839
and it has made him so much money.

00:14:32.839 --> 00:14:33.672
- It's authentically him.

00:14:33.672 --> 00:14:36.373
- It's so good and I really respect that.

00:14:37.521 --> 00:14:39.300
- Where are you at this point in your life

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:42.153
between money, legacy, the process?

00:14:43.563 --> 00:14:46.850
If I said 30-year old
you right now between,

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:49.950
generally in your stomach,
in your heart, in your gut,

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.673
about how much you're driven
by and how much you like

00:14:52.673 --> 00:14:55.353
the net win is around dollars

00:14:55.353 --> 00:14:59.770
is around being able to do your process

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:03.690
and around legacy, how are
you currently broken down

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:04.810
between 100% on those three?

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:06.660
- Well I don't have children yet

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.300
so maybe things will change
but I have enough that

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.713
I don't have to worry for maybe forever.

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:15.710
I thought I was really money motivated

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:17.560
but when you get all these opportunities

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:18.660
when you get to a little bit of a level

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.880
like we'll pay you 10 grand
to come here and do this

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.080
or whatever and I found
myself not wanting to do that

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:25.830
because I'm like ugh, that
sounds so boring to me,

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:26.730
I don't want to do that.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.640
So I guess that answers the question

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:29.990
of where I am a little bit.

00:15:31.141 --> 00:15:35.610
I mean like one time we
had a client do something

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:38.010
that we thought was an asshole
thing and we fired them

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.340
and we lost a quarter of a million dollars

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:40.830
and I slept like a baby.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:44.785
So I don't think I'm as
money motivated as I thought.

00:15:44.785 --> 00:15:47.810
- Do you think that's because
you checked some sort of box

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:49.744
for yourself along the way
versus 20 year old you?

00:15:49.744 --> 00:15:50.650
- No, here's what I say.

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:52.290
I had fuck you money when I had 10 grand.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:53.730
- [Gary] Yeah I get that.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:54.820
- You know what I'm saying?

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:55.653
- [Gary] Yeah, I do know that.

00:15:55.653 --> 00:15:56.920
- And then once I was like wait a minute--

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.230
- I still don't think
I've ever been more rich

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.886
than when I was doing
baseball card shows at 13

00:16:00.886 --> 00:16:03.600
because $3000, $4000 when you're 13

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:05.370
and you came from nothing,

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.010
I thought I had like a trillion.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:08.860
- I remember one day I sold hotdogs

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.880
and I had a grip of cash,
it was like a grand.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:11.723
- A grip.

00:16:11.723 --> 00:16:12.690
It was just a grip.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.010
- I called it a grip, let me get that grip

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:18.630
and it was because I had
like a jar here for tips

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.955
and I had like a grand in five
dollar bills and I was like

00:16:21.955 --> 00:16:22.980
this is fucking money.
- I made it.

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:23.873
- This is it.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.235
I was like we called it hood rich and--

00:16:27.235 --> 00:16:28.930
- Where did you grow up again?

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:29.950
- In St. Louis Missouri

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:31.730
but then I lived in Nashville, Tennessee

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.670
but I grew up with a
lot of like poor people

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:36.936
and you know that's why I talk like this.

00:16:36.936 --> 00:16:38.951
- Did you like it, did you think about--

00:16:38.951 --> 00:16:39.784
- I was you happy.

00:16:39.784 --> 00:16:42.620
So in Nashville I lived across
the street from the projects

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:43.791
and we would hang out all the time--

00:16:43.791 --> 00:16:45.246
- And how old were you then?

00:16:45.246 --> 00:16:46.936
- 19 to 21, 18 to 21.

00:16:46.936 --> 00:16:49.940
And I would just hang out
there with my buddy Rydell

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:52.030
and we would just chill and just sit on,

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.181
that was my happiest when I had nothing,

00:16:55.181 --> 00:16:56.621
which is weird, right?

00:16:56.621 --> 00:17:00.084
- No, I think it's not weird at all.

00:17:00.084 --> 00:17:01.540
And what'd you guys talk about?

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.736
Just like guy talk, girls,
sports, just random shit

00:17:04.736 --> 00:17:06.378
or would you talk about dreams?

00:17:06.378 --> 00:17:08.200
- Business, yeah.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:12.410
I mean he, this dude had
served 25 years in prison

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.426
and we would talk about that

00:17:14.426 --> 00:17:15.936
but just stupid stuff.

00:17:15.936 --> 00:17:17.550
But it's really fun.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.660
I think that when you come from not a lot,

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:21.500
which you know I had a
good family and everything

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.228
but when you're trying to start
on your own from scratch--

00:17:24.228 --> 00:17:25.830
- And do you have siblings?

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.000
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.010
I have siblings, older
brother and older sister.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:29.843
- [Gary] You were the youngest.

00:17:29.843 --> 00:17:30.738
- Mm-hmm yeah.

00:17:30.738 --> 00:17:34.000
I think when you start from not a lot,

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.850
it's actually a huge advantage

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:37.501
and you could be a lot happier that way.

00:17:37.501 --> 00:17:40.140
I think if you're born
into a wealthy family

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.480
the likelihood of like depression
might actually be higher.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.040
- [Gary] I think so.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:44.873
- [Sam] You know what I mean?

00:17:44.873 --> 00:17:47.080
- Based on our societal norms right now.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.331
So much of my passion about
like what I talk about

00:17:49.331 --> 00:17:52.621
and the content I put out
is to create empathy for,

00:17:52.621 --> 00:17:56.640
it's very hard you know,
I get it, I grew up in it.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.331
it's very hard for somebody
that doesn't have much

00:17:58.331 --> 00:18:01.730
to have empathy for
somebody who's driving a BMW

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.170
and has a Rolex but you know,

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:07.041
based on all my readings,
all my DMs I get,

00:18:07.041 --> 00:18:09.680
at this point in my life I
have hundreds of thousands

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:12.253
of interactions per month,
year, whatever it is.

00:18:12.253 --> 00:18:14.140
It's just a lot of interactions.

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:17.341
I see far more sadness in
people that had too much

00:18:17.341 --> 00:18:19.350
than in people had too little.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.970
I see envy in people
that don't have as much.

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:25.230
I see drive in people
that don't have as much.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:28.070
I see resentment in people
that don't have as much.

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:32.520
I see deep lostness in
people that had too much.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.099
You know?

00:18:34.099 --> 00:18:36.733
When people didn't have as much

00:18:36.733 --> 00:18:39.267
there is an inherent I'm
gonna pull myself out of this

00:18:39.267 --> 00:18:43.350
whether it's illegal,
legal, there's a drive.

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:46.207
It's really hard to be
hungry when you started fed.

00:18:46.207 --> 00:18:47.510
It just is.

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:48.630
- No and I agree.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.020
- And I have I have wealthy kids

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:51.670
and I think about it constantly.

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:52.980
- So let me ask you a question that

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:56.490
I bet a lot of people
wonder or maybe a fair bit,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.037
so you started this from nothing.

00:18:58.037 --> 00:18:58.870
- [Gary] Yep.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:00.520
- I started my business from nothing.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:04.650
I saw early on, even once we
got to like 25, 30 employees

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:06.777
once it got to like 10
million in revenue I was like,

00:19:06.777 --> 00:19:08.140
I don't want to operate

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:10.760
or I don't want to like be
the guy of this business.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:13.070
I want to just go start
from the bottom again

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:14.410
and start stuff within the company.

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:15.447
That's what I prefer.

00:19:15.447 --> 00:19:17.709
You seem like you would prefer that

00:19:17.709 --> 00:19:19.940
but it seems like you're
still like the guy in charge.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:21.270
- I do both.

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:23.780
The reason I have team Gary is
it is my little pirate ship.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:24.613
- How many people work there?

00:19:24.613 --> 00:19:25.500
- [Gary] Team Gary?

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:26.333
- Yeah.

00:19:26.333 --> 00:19:28.733
- 30 or so, right, feels right?

00:19:29.918 --> 00:19:33.503
So I have GaryVee, my team Gary,

00:19:34.353 --> 00:19:37.306
my K-Swiss collaboration,
my Empathy Wine--

